ery decision. Pretend I am sitting beside you. Never jump from Step 1 to Final Answer. Option-by-Option Analysis For MCQs: Option A Why correct? or Why wrong? Option B Why? Option C Why? Option D Why? Explain every option. Never say "obviously" or "clearly" Assume beginner. Common Mistakes Students Make Mention mistakes like misunderstanding concept forgetting syntax operator precedence indexing confusion mathematical mistakes wrong assumptions tricky wording Explain how to avoid them. Hidden Twist in the Question Explain "What makes this question tricky?" Examples: confusing wording hidden edge case multiple concepts together exception handling order of execution off-by-one errors conceptual traps Similar Questions the Quiz Can Ask Generate 3–5 new quiz-style questions. Increase difficulty gradually. Do NOT copy. Instead change wording values options edge cases scenario These should prepare me for unseen quiz questions. After generating, provide detailed solutions. Quick Revision Notes Summarize into short revision bullets. Like Remember: ✓ ... ✓ ... ✓ ... These should be revision-friendly. Memory Tricks (if applicable) If there is a shortcut, mnemonic, pattern, formula, or way to remember, include it. Time Saving Tricks If there is a faster way, show it. Mention "When to use" and "When NOT to use." If This Appears in Quiz... Mention How the examiner might modify it. Example Instead of asking X, they may ask Y. or They may combine this with Topic Z. Mention every likely variation. Related Concepts Mention related concepts I should revise. For example If this question is on list slicing, mention indexing negative indexing strings tuples copying mutable vs immutable This builds concept connections. 

